WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.760
Speaker 1: Welcome back to The David&nbsp;
Pakman Show. I'm producer Pat Forde,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:02.760 --> 00:00:07.860
filling in for David today. Tomorrow. Luke Beazley&nbsp;
is going to be hosting the program and then Farron&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.860 --> 00:00:12.900
Cousins will be filling in Wednesday, Thursday&nbsp;
before David makes his triumphant return to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.900 --> 00:00:18.780
show on Friday. Let's talk about the economy, the&nbsp;
issue that has plagued President Biden throughout&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.780 --> 00:00:25.440
much of his presidency, especially during the year&nbsp;
2022, was the high inflation rate peaking at 9.1%&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:31.320
in the summer. And of course, the reasons for the&nbsp;
high inflation are numerous. There was the supply&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.320 --> 00:00:36.780
chain issues, the Russian war in Ukraine and the&nbsp;
economic stimulus programs that came in response&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.780 --> 00:00:42.300
to the COVID 19 pandemic. And for the past year,&nbsp;
the Federal Reserve has been steadily hiking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.300 --> 00:00:48.360
interest rates to cool that inflation number down.&nbsp;
It does indeed appear to be working, as inflation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.360 --> 00:00:53.700
has been at a steady downtrend for eight or nine&nbsp;
months now, with it currently sitting at around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.700 --> 00:01:00.000
6%. However, there's still a ways to go before&nbsp;
we get to that 2 to 3% rate that the Fed wants to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:04.980
see. We've talked in the past about how you&nbsp;
want a little bit of inflation so that people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.980 --> 00:01:10.860
are incentivized to spend money now rather than&nbsp;
wait to purchase things in the future. Because if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.860 --> 00:01:14.760
people anticipate that things are going to be more&nbsp;
expensive in the future. They're going to want to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.760 --> 00:01:20.160
pay for them now. But of course, if the inflation&nbsp;
rate is too high, then people savings get depleted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.160 --> 00:01:25.620
and things get way too expensive, as we've seen&nbsp;
to some degree over the last year or two. Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.220 --> 00:01:32.340
what will necessarily happen as a result of trying&nbsp;
to tamp down on inflation and the higher interest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.340 --> 00:01:37.740
rates that come with it? Is the slowing of&nbsp;
job growth since businesses will have less&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.740 --> 00:01:44.040
money to hire new people and as borrowing money&nbsp;
becomes more expensive and that is finally what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.040 --> 00:01:48.840
we're seeing. It took a long time to get there,&nbsp;
but the latest job numbers from the month of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.840 --> 00:01:54.780
March 2023 show that we are finally getting to&nbsp;
a cooling down of the number of people getting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.780 --> 00:02:00.300
hired. It's still an increase, of course, month&nbsp;
to month as the population increases, but the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.300 --> 00:02:06.720
rate is slowing down now. Job creation slowed&nbsp;
to 236,000 for the month, almost reaching the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.720 --> 00:02:12.060
level that President Biden said would stabilize&nbsp;
the economy and prices. More Americans joined&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.060 --> 00:02:17.160
the labor force and wage gains fell slightly,&nbsp;
which could help to further cool inflation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.880 --> 00:02:24.000
The lower wage gains is yet another necessary&nbsp;
evil to calming down the rate of inflation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:29.580
It's one of these unfortunate necessities that is&nbsp;
seen by an economist, as you know what you have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.580 --> 00:02:34.800
to do to bring about a lower inflation rate.&nbsp;
The unemployment rate currently sits at 3.5%,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.800 --> 00:02:40.260
which is lower than what we saw under Trump. Lower&nbsp;
than what we saw under President Obama. Lower than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.260 --> 00:02:46.620
what we saw under President Bush. And given how&nbsp;
Trump would endlessly brag about the economy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.620 --> 00:02:51.120
he would love to have seen these numbers during&nbsp;
his tenure. Biden should be doing the exact&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.120 --> 00:02:56.640
same thing because 3.5% is essentially what we&nbsp;
would call full employment. Biden is cautioning,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.640 --> 00:03:00.840
however, that there is more work to do when it&nbsp;
comes to the economy to bring down high prices&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.840 --> 00:03:05.580
that are squeezing workers and families. Biden&nbsp;
previously stated that monthly job creation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.580 --> 00:03:13.440
needed to fall to around $150,000 of $150,&nbsp;
150,000 jobs. So there still is a ways to go.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.160 --> 00:03:20.700
I understand that this is maybe not the most&nbsp;
exciting story, but this comedown from the high&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.700 --> 00:03:25.560
inflation rate we always thought would take some&nbsp;
time. And indeed it has taken some time. It's been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.560 --> 00:03:30.060
eight or nine months at this point since inflation&nbsp;
has peaked and it was always going to come with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.060 --> 00:03:34.980
some sacrifices. When it comes to other economic&nbsp;
indicators like jobs numbers slowing down,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.980 --> 00:03:40.620
like wage growth slowing down, and as these&nbsp;
months go on and job creation cools and as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.620 --> 00:03:45.120
the inflation rate cools, we're definitely&nbsp;
hearing less and less from Republicans about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.120 --> 00:03:51.180
the economy overall. Perhaps that's why they're&nbsp;
so focused on these culture war issues these days,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.180 --> 00:03:56.640
talking about trans people and drag shows and&nbsp;
other topics like that because they just can't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.640 --> 00:04:00.540
seem to be making. They can't seem to make&nbsp;
salient points when it comes to gas prices&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.540 --> 00:04:05.280
anymore. They can't really talk about the stock&nbsp;
market as that has recovered to some degree. Can't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.280 --> 00:04:10.140
really talk about inflation since that's long&nbsp;
since peaked. And you know, over the last year,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.140 --> 00:04:14.400
they've just been talking about nonsense because&nbsp;
they really can't focus on these kitchen tables&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.400 --> 00:04:19.020
effect issues effectively like they were able&nbsp;
to about a year ago. And it's, of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.020 --> 00:04:23.040
impossible to predict where the economy is going&nbsp;
to go next. We don't know if things are going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.040 --> 00:04:26.940
have a turn around and we'll reverse course and&nbsp;
go in the opposite direction of where we want to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.940 --> 00:04:33.780
see things terribly difficult to do, to predict&nbsp;
the economy short to mid-term. But looking at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.780 --> 00:04:38.040
the data, looking at the trajectories of these&nbsp;
different things. My sense is that by the time the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.040 --> 00:04:43.680
2024 election rolls around, we could definitely&nbsp;
have inflation under control with job numbers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.680 --> 00:04:48.660
maybe not quite as good as they are now, but&nbsp;
still at a very healthy level. The Federal Reserve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.660 --> 00:04:53.580
will then have the green light to start lowering&nbsp;
interest rates again and things could end up being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.580 --> 00:04:59.580
in solid shape when it's time for voters to go to&nbsp;
the polls in 2024. And not only is that. Good for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.580 --> 00:05:04.080
us stakeholders who participate in the American&nbsp;
economy. It can also be good for President&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.080 --> 00:05:09.720
Biden's reelection chances if he indeed decides&nbsp;
to run, and it could make Republicans basically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.720 --> 00:05:14.340
unable to speak effectively about these kitchen&nbsp;
table issues and will have to resort to talking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.340 --> 00:05:20.040
about the culture war, whatever their new pet&nbsp;
issue is for the day. So inflation cooling down,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.040 --> 00:05:23.940
jobs number is cooling down along with it.&nbsp;
And, of course, we'll continue to follow it.

